From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0147-batch-cpu-readback-ranges.patch

Source: 0147-batch-cpu-readback-ranges.patch
buffer_cache: batch CPU-readback ranges into one GPU wait, behind a switch

0360f3045 and ef3a246d1, batching from 23d33887a).

With EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH=0 a guest CPU read of GPU-written memory no longer marks the pages
Preflushable, which downloads them at every later fence. The read trap stays in use: each
read flushes on demand, the ranges read so far are remembered, and the next flush
downloads what the GPU changed in all of them with one wait. Lichii measured Finish
calls going from 1,184 to 70 a second in Kokiri Forest, with the range cache of 0145.

Changed from the PR:
- The default stays as it is here: Preflushable marks, for every title. The PR turns them
  off for all Switch 2 titles, but it has not been run in a game in its final form, and
  every console read would then depend on the trap path. EDEN_NX2_PREFLUSH=0 in env.txt
  tries it. Without it the only change is the clip in DownloadMemory below.
- DownloadRangesMemory takes our refused-staging path (TryDownloadStagingBuffer, then
  DownloadInPieces), as DownloadBuffersMemory does, so a refused staging buffer does not
  end the session here.
- read_trap_tracker.h and its test are left out: the class only wraps the RangeSet for
  that test. The test file differs here.

DownloadMemory now clips each buffer's download to the buffer. The memory tracker
is not per buffer, so a range over two buffers could ask a copy past the end of one.

On the console the trap is software: no fastmem arena, so ArmReadTraps bumps the read
generation, which drops each core's cached area, and the read goes to OnCPURead.
Run 38's out of memory came from fence downloads (CreateStagingBuffer from
CommitAsyncFlushesHigh), so the switch may also lower staging use. That is a guess.

